      "id": "https://www.archdaily.com/1186222/from-relic-to-infrastructure-can-indias-historic-water-systems-work-again",
      "url": "https://www.archdaily.com/1186222/from-relic-to-infrastructure-can-indias-historic-water-systems-work-again",
      "title": "[ArchDaily] From Relic to Infrastructure: Can India's Historic Water Systems Work Again?",
      "content_html": "<img src=\"https://images.adsttc.com/media/images/6abf/3d4c/87ec/5401/8809/f83c/large_jpg/the-water-beneath-the-monument-can-heritage-become-infrastructure-again_11.jpg?1790917976\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\"/>\n<p>India's stepwells are usually encountered after their useful lives have ended. They are photographed, restored and interpreted as <a href=\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12685-026-00386-z?utm_medium=website&amp;utm_source=archdaily.com\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">evidence of a water culture that once existed.</a> Beneath the carved stone and descending stairs is a practical question: what happens when a structure built to manage water is put back to work? As Indian cities face water stress, seasonal rainfall and pressure on municipal systems, <a href=\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s43238-026-00248-w?utm_medium=website&amp;utm_source=archdaily.com\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">researchers and restoration efforts are examining whether traditional water structures can function again.</a></p>",
      "date_published": "2026-10-08T07:30:00+00:00",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "ArchDaily",
          "url": "https://www.archdaily.com"
        }
      ],
      "image": "https://images.adsttc.com/media/images/6abf/3d4c/87ec/5401/8809/f83c/large_jpg/the-water-beneath-the-monument-can-heritage-become-infrastructure-again_11.jpg?1790917976",
      "_score": 50,
      "_quality": 68,
      "_relevance": 60,
      "_local_score": 0,
      "_content_type": "feature"
    },
    {
      "id": "https://www.archdaily.com/1185941/from-desert-heat-to-northern-winters-how-container-architecture-adapts-across-the-united-states",
      "url": "https://www.archdaily.com/1185941/from-desert-heat-to-northern-winters-how-container-architecture-adapts-across-the-united-states",
      "title": "[ArchDaily] From Desert Heat to Northern Winters: How Container Architecture Adapts Across the United States",
      "content_html": "<img src=\"https://images.adsttc.com/media/images/6abf/d32e/90e2/9d01/89d2/dac9/large_jpg/from-desert-heat-to-northern-winters-how-container-architecture-adapts-across-the-united-states_8.jpg?1790956349\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\"/>\n<p>Far from simply transporting goods from A to B, shipping containers have become a go-to building material across the US, forming the basis of everything from tiny homes to container pools, site offices to workshops. But given the size of the country, and the range of climates found within its borders, the approach to container architecture can vary significantly from state to state, even county to county.</p>",
